<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1648–1695</span> · Also known as Juana Inés de Asbaje</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz was a Hieronymite nun, poet and scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> and the outstanding writer of the Spanish baroque in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Her works include the long poem Primero sueño, plays, love poetry and the Respuesta a Sor Filotea of 1691, a defence of women's right to learning. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> while nursing victims of an epidemic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sor-juana-ines-de-la-cruz.jpg" alt="Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguel Cabrera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sor_Juana_by_Miguel_Cabrera_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
